from author import *
from rag_api import lab
lab(21,'allocation','Coordinate specialist budgets and merge conflicts',
'''Parallel specialists can inspect independent evidence, but they share one user task and one resource budget. Giving each specialist the full budget multiplies the possible total cost. Shared state needs an explicit merge policy and evidence provenance; last-writer-wins can hide a conflicting conclusion.\n\nThe supervisor should integrate findings rather than merely concatenate answers. If a log specialist identifies automation timing while a history specialist suggests a product regression, preserve the disagreement and identify the additional evidence needed.''',
'''Assign task IDs, scoped inputs, per-task ceilings and a shared remaining allowance. Reserve budget before delegation and settle actual usage afterward. Keep results as typed records with evidence IDs, status and uncertainty. Deduplicate source IDs without discarding which specialist used them.\n\nIndependent reads can run concurrently; dependent analysis and side effects remain ordered. A failed child task returns an explicit failed result, and cancellation propagates to all pending work. The parent cannot mark missing results successful.''',
'''Two specialists each spend eight calls against a ten-call parent limit. Another specialist writes a conclusion into shared state while a second overwrites it without conflict review. These failures may be invisible in the final prose.\n\nTest budget reservation, duplicate task IDs, late results after cancellation and conflicting evidence. Compare total steps and latency against a single-agent baseline before retaining the more complex design.''',
'''Release evidence includes task scopes, allocations, actual usage and integrated conclusions. Measure whether specialization improves task completion or evidence coverage enough to justify coordination cost.\n\nFor SDETs, orchestration tests should use deterministic specialist results first. This isolates coordination correctness from model behavior and makes race conditions and failure propagation reproducible.''',
"def reserve(remaining,requested):\n    if not 0<=requested<=remaining:raise ValueError('budget')\n    return remaining-requested",
"def allocate(total,requests):\n    if total<0 or any(r<0 for r in requests) or sum(requests)>total:raise ValueError('budget')\n    return total-sum(requests)",
"assert allocate(10,[3,4])==3\ntry:allocate(10,[8,8]);assert False\nexcept ValueError:pass","def allocate(total,requests):\n    return 0",
('What should happen to contradictory specialist findings?',['Preserve the conflict and seek discriminating evidence','Keep whichever answer arrived last','Average their prose'],0,'Coordination requires evidence-aware integration, not blind overwrite.'),['Budget reservation','Conflict resolution'])
lab(27,'output','Output handling: redaction, rendering and policy layers',
'''Model output is untrusted content. Returning JSON does not make embedded strings safe for HTML, logs, SQL or tools. Context-specific encoding prevents output from becoming executable markup, while authorization controls which facts or actions may be produced. Moderation is another classifier with false positives and false negatives.\n\nA sensitive value may appear in logs or traces even when the final answer is redacted. Apply privacy rules across artifacts, not only the user-facing response.''',
'''Render text through React\'s escaped text nodes and sanitize generated SVG before insertion. Escape model/log text in HTML reports. Redact sensitive structured attributes recursively, and store permitted raw evidence only in a separate controlled location with retention rules.\n\nKeep schema validation, domain evidence checks, content policy and output encoding separate. A moderation pass cannot authorize a cross-tenant read, and HTML escaping cannot make an unsupported factual claim correct.''',
'''A report directly interpolates <script> from a synthetic failure log. A shallow redactor removes api_key at the top level but misses it inside provider.request.headers. A deny-list phrase check misses paraphrased injection.\n\nTest nested structures, case-insensitive sensitive field names, malformed output and dangerous markup. Verify that allowed ordinary content still renders and that blocked content produces a visible controlled outcome.''',
'''The shared report generator uses escaped HTML from the same JSON artifact. The diagram editor combines strict Mermaid settings with sanitized SVG. These are rendering controls, while tool and tenant policies enforce operational boundaries.\n\nSDETs should identify which layer each test protects. Passing one layer\'s test is not evidence that another layer\'s policy holds; retain separate verdicts for privacy, rendering, authorization and semantic support.''',
"import html\nassert html.escape('<script>')=='&lt;script&gt;'",
"def redact_nested(value):\n    blocked={'api_key','authorization','password','token'}\n    if isinstance(value,dict):return {k:('[REDACTED]' if k.lower() in blocked else redact_nested(v)) for k,v in value.items()}\n    if isinstance(value,list):return [redact_nested(v) for v in value]\n    return value",
"assert redact_nested({'x':[{'API_KEY':'s'}]})=={'x':[{'API_KEY':'[REDACTED]'}]}\nassert redact_nested({'task':'F1'})=={'task':'F1'}","def redact_nested(value):\n    return value",
('Can HTML escaping enforce tenant authorization?',['No: it protects rendering, not access rights','Yes: all escaped content is authorized','Only for JSON strings'],0,'Encoding and permission checks protect different boundaries.'),['Output encoding','Recursive redaction'])
lab(29,'dependencies','Generated dependencies: import checks and sandbox boundaries',
'''A generated function can rely on a nonexistent library or an obsolete method even when the algorithm sounds correct. Dependency manifests, lockfiles, import smoke tests and official API checks establish compatibility. Static inspection can identify suspicious calls, but it is not a complete sandbox.\n\nGenerated candidates must run without production credentials, with resource limits and controlled network/filesystem access. A model\'s instruction to behave safely does not isolate the process.''',
'''Parse Python with ast before execution, review imports and reject unsupported dependencies. Run type/compile checks and focused tests in a disposable environment. Lock accepted versions and inspect transitive dependency advisories.\n\nAST-based rules can detect direct eval/exec or os.system calls for review, but aliases, dynamic imports and indirect effects can evade simple patterns. Use operating-system/container isolation for enforcement and label lexical/static checks as screening.''',
'''A test suite never imports the generated module, so a hallucinated import remains hidden. A deny list blocks the string subprocess while a dynamically assembled command still executes. Installing every generated dependency expands supply-chain risk.\n\nTest import failure and API mismatch explicitly. Keep an allowlisted dependency set for candidate tasks, and inspect a deliberately evasive example to understand why screening cannot guarantee containment.''',
'''Acceptance combines compatible dependencies, meaningful behavior tests, reviewed effects and actual isolation appropriate to the environment. The browser worker limits runtime and keeps credentials absent; local code needs its own controlled execution environment.\n\nFor SDETs, distinguish test discovery from execution: a file can exist without its assertions ever running. Verify a known failing candidate makes the pipeline fail before trusting generated-code coverage.''',
"import ast\ntree=ast.parse('import imaginary_package')\nimports=[n.names[0].name for n in ast.walk(tree) if isinstance(n,ast.Import)]\nassert imports==['imaginary_package']",
"def imports_in(code):\n    import ast\n    tree=ast.parse(code);out=[]\n    for node in ast.walk(tree):\n        if isinstance(node,ast.Import):out.extend(n.name.split('.')[0] for n in node.names)\n        elif isinstance(node,ast.ImportFrom) and node.module:out.append(node.module.split('.')[0])\n    return sorted(set(out))",
"assert imports_in('import os, json\\nfrom pathlib import Path')==['json','os','pathlib']\nassert imports_in('x=1')==[]","def imports_in(code):\n    return []",
('Is a simple AST deny list a complete execution sandbox?',['No: it is screening with known blind spots','Yes: Python cannot call indirect functions','Only if the prompt is strict'],0,'Static patterns cannot enforce all runtime effects; isolation provides a separate boundary.'),['Dependency lock','Sandbox'])
lab(32,'deployment','Container smoke tests: data paths, ports and persistence',
'''A local import working in a virtual environment does not prove the container image works. Working directories, module paths, user permissions and dataset locations can differ. The shared service uses a configured dataset root so its domain code does not depend on the checkout\'s parent directory layout.\n\nBuild context determines which files are available to COPY. The repository-root context includes local-labs and datasets; a narrow fastapi-only context omits the shared quality package.''',
'''Build with the supplied root-context Dockerfile, run as UID 10001 and bind uvicorn to 0.0.0.0. Probe health/readiness and exercise every domain endpoint. Verify that the dataset path is readable under the non-root user and that credentials enter only at runtime.\n\nFor Qdrant server mode, restart the service with its named volume and repeat an authorized query. Read-only API filesystems require temporary/runtime artifacts to use explicitly permitted storage rather than the image layer.''',
'''A Dockerfile copies ../datasets outside its build context and fails only in CI. A service imports the installed fastapi library instead of its app module. A relative data path silently loads no documents and produces abstention for every query.\n\nTest a known retrieval question inside the container, not merely process startup. Check shutdown, port mapping and persistence. If Docker is unavailable in the development environment, record that execution limitation instead of claiming a smoke pass.''',
'''Acceptance evidence includes image identity, non-root identity, endpoint results, dataset path and restart behavior. Use the exact tested image for deployment and preserve its compatible rollback configuration.\n\nFor SDETs, environment tests should prove that a hand-computed fixture behaves the same inside and outside the container. This catches packaging defects independently of live model behavior.''',
"docker compose up --build\n# From another terminal, probe /health, /ready and /chat\n# Confirm query 'retry HTTP 429' returns the authorized retry citation",
"def required_build_files(files):\n    required={'local-labs/quality','local-labs/fastapi','datasets'}\n    return required<=set(files)",
"assert required_build_files(['local-labs/quality','local-labs/fastapi','datasets'])\nassert not required_build_files(['local-labs/fastapi'])","def required_build_files(files):\n    return False",
('Which probe detects a missing corpus that health alone misses?',['A known retrieval question with expected citation','Only process ID existence','The image size'],0,'An end-to-end fixture verifies data packaging and serving behavior.'),['Build context','Container smoke test'])
lab(33,'artifacts','GitHub Actions: failure artifacts and protected evaluation modes',
'''CI should make failures reviewable. Unit results, browser traces, case reports and version manifests are artifacts that explain why a gate failed. Uploading them only on success loses the most useful evidence. Secret-bearing live evaluation requires a distinct protected execution context from forked contributions.\n\nDeterministic fixture checks can run without credentials on every change. Live model-quality measurements should use configured budgets and stable cases, with mode prominently recorded.''',
'''Order install, content integrity, lint, unit/API checks, build and browser tests. Run the local evaluator and preserve its exit code. Upload reports with always() so a failed case still leaves its JSON/HTML evidence. Restrict workflow permissions to the job\'s actual needs.\n\nDeploy the tested build artifact with the configured GitHub Pages base path. Content JSON changes must trigger validation just as code changes do. A separate completion-depth gate prevents structural validity from being mistaken for finished curriculum.''',
'''A workflow runs an evaluator but adds continue-on-error, making the release green. A fork is allowed to access provider secrets. A path filter runs only for src/**/*.tsx and skips broken lesson JSON.\n\nTest the workflow logic with a known failing case and inspect that deployment cannot proceed. Review secret scope and artifact redaction, and record which checks were fixture-only versus live.''',
'''Acceptance includes a red gate that retained its artifacts and a corrected green gate using the same cases. Workflow YAML is a design artifact until its checks actually run in the intended environment.\n\nFor SDETs, connect every advertised guarantee to a required check and ensure missing evidence fails visibly. This is the difference between a report that informs a release and one that controls it.''',
"# Required local equivalents\nnpm run content:check\nnpm run lint\nnpm test\nnpm run build\npython local-labs/evals/runner.py",
"def artifact_policy(status):\n    return {'upload':status in {'passed','failed','cancelled'},'deploy':status=='passed'}",
"assert artifact_policy('failed')=={'upload':True,'deploy':False}\nassert artifact_policy('passed')['deploy']","def artifact_policy(status):\n    return {}",
('Should failed evaluation artifacts still be uploaded?',['Yes, while preserving a failed job verdict','No, failure evidence should disappear','Only if deployment already happened'],0,'Failure evidence supports diagnosis without allowing release.'),['Protected secret','Failure artifact'])
lab(34,'correlation','Trace correlation and safe telemetry assertions',
'''A trace is useful only when its spans explain the user task. Queue, retrieval, provider, validation and tool spans should share context while recording their own durations and statuses. A random request ID in every log without shared linkage cannot reconstruct the task.\n\nTelemetry is also a data surface. Prompt text, authorization headers and evidence from restricted tenants should not become broadly accessible trace attributes.''',
'''The local OpenTelemetry example emits a parent release-evaluation span and a child deterministic-cases span with dataset and mode attributes. Add domain spans around dependency calls, propagate trace context and mark failures at the responsible stage.\n\nKeep safe scalar attributes and controlled artifact references. Redact structured sensitive fields recursively; use a dedicated protected store for raw evidence when the product requires it. Configure retention and reader access independently from application access.''',
'''A provider failure is swallowed and the span remains successful. Retry attempts have no parent, preventing correlation. A nested header includes a secret despite a top-level redactor.\n\nTest required span names, parent IDs, error statuses, attempt counts and forbidden attributes using an in-memory exporter. Preserve a synthetic canary test so privacy regressions in telemetry are visible.''',
'''Acceptance evidence shows a complete trace for a normal request and an injected failure, with no canary or credential attributes. Version IDs connect the trace to the evaluation run and release manifest.\n\nSDETs can make observability assertions deterministic even when model quality is probabilistic. A useful trace explains the first failed boundary and the visible fallback without exposing sensitive content.''',
"python local-labs/frameworks/tracing.py\n# Inspect parent_span_id, dataset.version and evaluation.passed in output",
"def safe_attributes(attrs):\n    forbidden={'authorization','api_key','password','raw_prompt'}\n    return not any(k.lower() in forbidden for k in attrs)",
"assert safe_attributes({'dataset.version':'v1'})\nassert not safe_attributes({'Authorization':'secret'})\nassert not safe_attributes({'raw_prompt':'sensitive'})","def safe_attributes(attrs):\n    return False",
('What links a trace to a reproducible quality experiment?',['Dataset, prompt/model/index and run identities','Raw passwords','Only the console timestamp'],0,'Version identities connect execution evidence to its exact inputs.'),['Trace correlation','Telemetry redaction'])
lab(35,'budget','Enforce task budgets before hidden work grows',
'''A task budget bounds total attempts and their resource use, not only the final accepted response. Retries, discarded generations, judge calls and agent tools all contribute. A request can stay within each component\'s local limit while exceeding the user\'s total allowance.\n\nEstimates and measured usage serve different purposes. Reserve estimated capacity before work, then reconcile actual usage returned by the provider. Rates are dated configuration; this course does not embed current pricing as a permanent fact.''',
'''Track remaining input/output token allowance, call count, wall time and estimated currency cost in task state. Refuse or degrade before beginning work that cannot fit the budget. Include cached-token and reasoning usage according to provider accounting rather than guessing.\n\nCalculate cost per accepted task over all attempts. Compare routing and caching variants on the same dataset with quality and privacy constraints held fixed. A low billed cost that increases wrong automated decisions is not a successful optimization.''',
'''An agent gives each retry a fresh token limit and never reconciles total usage. A judge runs on every intermediate draft, multiplying spend. A cache uses tenant-free keys and saves cost by leaking evidence.\n\nTest budget exhaustion before and after a provider response, duplicated retries and negative/invalid usage values. Ensure the visible status explains budget termination rather than claiming a completed investigation.''',
'''Acceptance includes bounded work, dated rate configuration and measured usage artifacts. Report total spend, accepted tasks, abstentions and review load together.\n\nFor SDETs, budgets are deterministic state invariants that can be tested with synthetic usage events. Live provider measurements then establish actual operating cost under the declared workload.''',
"remaining=1000\nusage_events=[200,300,400]\nassert sum(usage_events)<=remaining",
"def remaining_budget(total,usage):\n    if total<0 or any(u<0 for u in usage):raise ValueError('usage')\n    return max(0,total-sum(usage))",
"assert remaining_budget(100,[20,30])==50\nassert remaining_budget(100,[80,40])==0\ntry:remaining_budget(100,[-1]);assert False\nexcept ValueError:pass","def remaining_budget(total,usage):\n    return total",
('Which attempts belong in cost per accepted task?',['All attempts, including failures and retries','Only the final success','Only the cheapest model'],0,'Useful-task economics include the work spent on unsuccessful outcomes.'),['Usage reconciliation','Task budget'])
lab(36,'workload','Load-test interpretation and coordinated omission',
'''A closed-loop client waits for responses before sending more work, which is useful for bounded service smoke tests but can hide queue pressure during slow periods. An arrival-rate workload schedules requests independently of completions and better exposes overload, while requiring careful admission and resource controls.\n\nProvider TTFT requires streaming event timing. A local extractive endpoint returning one JSON payload cannot establish generation TTFT or tokens-per-second performance.''',
'''Run the supplied bounded load client and label its mode as local extractive service. Record count, concurrency, errors, successful throughput and raw latency samples. Use a k6 constant-arrival-rate experiment for capacity testing when the environment supports it.\n\nInclude long and short inputs, cache states and slow dependencies. Separate queue delay from service execution and define whether rejected/time-out requests count in each reported statistic. Keep quality checks under load so fast invalid responses do not look successful.''',
'''Increasing concurrency lowers reported latency because failed requests are dropped. A tiny fixture endpoint is compared with a live model and described as a model speedup. A benchmark reports only p50 and misses a severe p99 tail.\n\nTest the metric calculation with known samples, inspect errors and repeat the same workload. State the client scheduling model and environment so a reader can interpret its limits.''',
'''Acceptance states workload, mode, success rate, latency tails and throughput. A capacity claim requires the production-like dependency path and a suitable arrival model, not only a smoke script.\n\nSDETs should connect load thresholds to user outcomes and identify where saturation occurs. Preserve failure samples and trace IDs rather than averaging the service into a single favorable number.''',
"python local-labs/performance/load.py --count 50 --concurrency 5\n# This is a bounded closed-loop local-service measurement",
"def throughput(completed,seconds):\n    if completed<0 or seconds<=0:raise ValueError('bounds')\n    return completed/seconds",
"assert throughput(50,10)==5\ntry:throughput(1,0);assert False\nexcept ValueError:pass","def throughput(completed,seconds):\n    return 0",
('Can a non-streaming extractive fixture measure live-model TTFT?',['No: it measures a different execution path','Yes: every first byte is a generated token','Only at high concurrency'],0,'TTFT requires the actual generation stream and its token events.'),['Arrival-rate workload','Closed-loop load'])
lab(37,'matrix','Turn the failure matrix into executable recovery contracts',
'''A failure matrix names each dependency fault, detection signal, allowed recovery and visible result. Provider timeouts, malformed responses, retrieval absence and unsafe tool requests require different handling. Retrying everything treats deterministic invalidity as if it were transient.\n\nRecovery must preserve the original trust boundary and budget. A fallback with broader access or unsupported claims turns a reliability mechanism into a security or quality regression.''',
'''Create one fixture per fault and assert final status, attempt limit, retained evidence and effects. The provider adapter uses an outer async deadline; the tool state machine stops repeated signatures; retrieval absence returns explicit abstention.\n\nFor combined faults, test shared capacity and cancellation. Recheck permissions and approvals after a resumed task. Keep error categories stable in the public API while retaining safe internal diagnostic references.''',
'''A malformed provider response is retried until the deadline but the public response still says success. A fallback copies another tenant\'s cached answer. A stopped agent\'s old partial draft is presented as a verified fix.\n\nInspect actual state and effect counts, not just error wording. Test that resources are released and that the next healthy request can succeed after the fault is removed.''',
'''Acceptance records each fault, expected and observed outcome, trace and recovery evidence. A declared tolerance is supported only by an executed case.\n\nSDETs can run this suite without paid model calls by injecting the adapter/tool boundary. Live chaos experiments, when appropriate, add external-dependency evidence but do not replace deterministic recovery tests.''',
"faults={'timeout':'unavailable','missing_context':'abstained','unsafe_write':'denied','loop':'stopped'}\nassert len(set(faults.values()))==4",
"def failure_contract(fault,observed):\n    expected={'timeout':'unavailable','missing_context':'abstained','unsafe_write':'denied','loop':'stopped'}\n    return fault in expected and observed==expected[fault]",
"assert failure_contract('loop','stopped')\nassert not failure_contract('unsafe_write','complete')\nassert not failure_contract('new','retry')","def failure_contract(fault,observed):\n    return False",
('What must a reliability fallback preserve?',['Authorization, truthfulness and total budget','A promise of success at any cost','Unrestricted alternate tools'],0,"Degradation changes capability, not the system\'s safety and evidence obligations."),['Failure matrix','Recovery contract'])
lab(38,'manifest','Artifact manifests and compatibility-aware rollback',
'''An experiment\'s result depends on a compatible set of artifacts. Model, tokenizer, embedding/index contract, prompt, schema, dataset and evaluator versions form a release identity. A mutable latest alias cannot reproduce all of these inputs.\n\nRollback is a tested operation restoring a compatible set, not a prose instruction to use the old model. Permission revocations and deleted sources must remain enforced even when an older index generation is restored.''',
'''Write a manifest after gates pass, linking immutable hashes or revisions and the tested image. Register metrics and artifacts in MLflow or portable JSON. Preserve a rollback target and test a known fixture through that configuration.\n\nDistinguish training artifacts from serving configuration and evaluator configuration. A provider may expose an alias rather than internal weights; record the alias, request date and observed behavior while acknowledging that limit.''',
'''A model rollback retains the new label ordering and swaps categories. A prompt rollback retains an incompatible evidence schema. An older index exposes a document whose access was revoked after the index was built.\n\nTest compatibility before switching traffic and apply current authorization at query time. Keep a manifest completeness check and a rollback smoke case in the release pipeline.''',
'''Acceptance includes manifest completeness, dataset/case identity, artifact compatibility and observed rollback behavior. Drift monitoring connects post-release failures to external provider or data changes.\n\nFor SDETs, reproducibility claims should be falsifiable: a reader can rerun fixture cases and obtain the same deterministic results from the declared artifact set. Live model repeatability is measured, not assumed.''',
"manifest={'code':'sha','dataset':'ci-v1','prompt':'p1','model':'m1','index':'i1','schema':'s1','evaluation':'e1'}\nassert all(manifest.values())",
"def manifest_complete(manifest):\n    required={'code','dataset','prompt','model','index','schema','evaluation'}\n    return required<=set(manifest) and all(isinstance(manifest[k],str) and bool(manifest[k]) for k in required)",
"assert manifest_complete(dict.fromkeys(['code','dataset','prompt','model','index','schema','evaluation'],'v1'))\nassert not manifest_complete({'code':'v1'})","def manifest_complete(manifest):\n    return False",
('Should an old index bypass current revoked permissions during rollback?',['No: current authorization still applies','Yes: rollback restores all historical access','Only for relevant documents'],0,'Artifact rollback does not revoke current security policy.'),['Artifact compatibility','Rollback smoke test'])
lab(39,'lora','LoRA initialization, training and acceptance are separate stages',
'''LoRA parameterizes an update as low-rank factors rather than training every base weight. For an output-by-input matrix, factors with rank r use r times input+output trainable parameters. This reduces trainable state but does not prove better task behavior or reduce every inference cost automatically.\n\nInitializing an adapter and running a forward pass are integration checks. Fine-tuning additionally optimizes it on reviewed examples, and acceptance additionally evaluates a held-out task and safety dataset.''',
'''The optional PEFT script requires a chosen compatible public checkpoint and target projection names. It builds a sequence-classification adapter, checks output shape and saves the initialized adapter with an explicit untrained label. Use the real PyTorch training lab to understand optimizer, loss and holdout mechanics before extending the adapter script.\n\nRecord training examples, base/tokenizer revisions, rank, alpha, dropout and adapter artifact. Use grouped splits and compare prompting/RAG/baseline alternatives on the same independent evaluation cases.''',
'''A developer presents initialized adapter weights as a fine-tuned model. Another selects target module names from a different architecture and attaches no useful trainable projections. Training loss falls while test performance declines.\n\nInspect trainable parameter counts and gradient flow, run saved/reloaded equivalence and evaluate unknown/rare cases. Check data contamination and privacy behavior before serving an adapted model.''',
'''Acceptance distinguishes initialized, trained and evaluated artifacts in both filenames and reports. A trained adapter is promoted only if held-out gains justify its maintenance and rollback cost.\n\nSDETs should demand executable evidence for each stage. Framework calls and decreasing training loss establish mechanics, while independent task and safety tests establish whether the adapted behavior meets the product contract.''',
"python local-labs/deep-learning/lora.py\n# Requires FINETUNE_MODEL and LORA_TARGETS\n# Output explicitly identifies an initialized, untrained adapter",
"def adapter_status(initialized,trained,evaluated):\n    if evaluated and not trained:raise ValueError('evaluation requires trained artifact')\n    if trained and not initialized:raise ValueError('training requires initialization')\n    return 'evaluated' if evaluated else 'trained' if trained else 'initialized' if initialized else 'absent'",
"assert adapter_status(True,False,False)=='initialized'\nassert adapter_status(True,True,True)=='evaluated'\ntry:adapter_status(True,False,True);assert False\nexcept ValueError:pass","def adapter_status(initialized,trained,evaluated):\n    return 'trained'",
('What does an adapter forward-pass smoke test establish?',['Integration and shape compatibility, not fine-tuning quality','Held-out accuracy','Guaranteed privacy'],0,'Initialization and inference mechanics are distinct from trained, independently evaluated behavior.'),['Adapter','Trainable parameters'])
